mate of the 1630s and why that  work then takes  this different form and dicots other  writings so I'm sort of teased out here  again the beginnings of the new science  and its connection to these older ideas  of philosophy and how it Beavs the way  for other ways of thinking both  scientifically and in terms of the  conflict between religion and science  and the final chapter is on John  Milton's Paradise Lost and I come to  this text that everybody knows in reads  in school in college and in school by  really asking the question why should we  write a poem about Genesis in the late  1660s you know this is well pastor  Copernicus it's prostate card where in  the world of the new science why go back  to this creation story and so I argue  here that Paradise Lost is really a  rewriting of Genesis for a post  Cartesian age then what happens when we  have this new version of world Milton is  the first poet one might say to imagine  an infinite universe where Milton's  universe is expansive spacious infinite  and how does that square with narratives  of Genesis that have seemed to be in  conflict with this new idea of the  cosmos and of the world not just  understood as our earth but understood  as our universe so I look there again at  the kind of imaginative play that's  required in some ways to hold on both to  the idea of religion and create creation  stories but also what it means to kind  of embrace the new imagination of an  infinite universe so through the course  of your studying these time periods can  you pinpoint or not necessarily pinpoint  but approximate when we finally kind of  had a close approximation to what we  think of as the world now that's a great  question um so I think it's a two-step  process I think by the end of the  sixteenth century we have a pretty good  sense of our world as an earth as a kind  of Geographic idea and ice and that sort  of roughly corresponds to the rise of  the world atlas as a genre a new kind of  book and I think the second phase of  that is the expansion of that Geographic  concept out of the kind of sphere of the  earth into the into the cosmos and that  is what we see in the 17th century so I  think that they're these kind of two  watershed moments where for the first  time people understand the world to be a  combination literally of land  water I mean in geography before the  late 16th century you think of land and  water as two separate things you don't  think of them as kind of a hybrid  integrated body and that conceptual  shift is the kind of first important  moment and then to kind of push that  imagination outward into the universe's  the second and so one nice example of  this is Columbus become such an  important figure in the narratives of  discovery right I mean he's the one who  crossed the ocean and that image of  Columbus the discoverer is an image  that's adopted by Galileo but he speaks  of his own discovery of the you know of  Jupiter and the moons of Jupiter and  that sense of expanding this terrestrial  metaphor of you know sailors going to  other places into the universe right I  mean that you think of these angels and  Milton's decks traversing space as being  sailors across the oceans right I mean  it's the kind of import of one kind of  metaphor into another sphere that also  suggests quite literally a kind of  imaginative expansion so what you  conclude ultimately in your book and  what would you like  readers to take away from your book so I  think two things I mean this is in some  ways this is a descriptive history it's  an attempt to kind of recover a way of  thinking that we have come to take for  granted completely and so I I guess I  might I would say that two takeaways I  mean one is this is the book that  responds to a transformative moment now  we are I think very much in a kind of  similar moment of trying to tease out  how that idea of world might itself be  changing I think much of the emphasis on  an interest in globalization comes out  of this this sort of attempt to make  sense of our own moment and the kinds of  radical changes that we see and part of  what I want to suggest is that our sense  of flux today when we confronted with  the idea of globalization itself has a  history I mean if we go back and think  of that problem itself historically we  might recognize some of the strategies  that were used you know four hundred  years ago that paradoxically we find  ourselves using so I end the book  actually by thinking about Google Earth  and the fascination that people have  with using Google Earth as being  analogous to the excitement of people  using the first maps in the world and  both atlases right what does that mean  of our own sense of ourselves in space  and so I I really do believe that one of  the things that historical study  offers us is that sense of reflecting on  where we are now  the second really important thing take  away I think from the book is that we  have spent so much time thinking about  concepts like world in largely what I  would call social science terms in terms  of economics politics and and that's  really important and of course  crucial in terms the way our lives are  structured what we have lost and what I  think that someone like me working in  comparative literature can bring to the  question is the kind of imaginative  conceptual work that underlies the  making of these big concepts and so my  book bases a lot of emphasis on the kind  of imaginative and speculative leaps  that people have to make to get from one  theoretical paradigm to another but it  not just material sort of shifts of  institutions and nations but people have  to actually imagine themselves there  first right I mean science doesn't just  happen it's because some people someone  actually sat down and thought I can do  this differently let me think about how  and there are lot of mistakes along the  way so far what my book wants to recover  is that sense of excitement the kind of  importance of the imagination the  importance of conceptual speculation all  of which we now think of as being kind  of in the humanities or kind of related  to the arts and this is a book that  really talks about the kind of  integrated work that the arts and the  sciences do together  the importance of speculation and the  imagination for diplomacy and politics  right and we have separated those two  things out and part of the takeaway of  the book I think is how much we have to  gain from going back and kind of  bringing those relationships back  together  very good thank you so much for being  here with us today thank you so much for  having me okay for more information  about professor or Ramachandran and her  work please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale
 

